---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = "How to Find Scholarships You Actually Qualify For | CloudBridge";
const description = "Where scholarship money really is, whether you need top grades, and when to apply — a practical guide to funding your studies abroad.";
---
<Layout title={title} description={description}>

<section class="py-10 bg-white border-b border-black/5">
  <div class="max-w-container mx-auto px-4 md:px-8">
    <p class="crumb !text-ink/40"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/blogs">Blogs</a> / Find Scholarships</p>
  </div>
</section>
<article class="py-10 md:py-14">
  <div class="max-w-container mx-auto px-4 md:px-8 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="course-badge">Scholarships</p>
    <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-bridge-950 leading-tight">How to Find Scholarships You Actually Qualify For</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink/50">Updated July 2026 · 8 min read</p>
    <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
      <img src="/assets/blog/scholarships.jpg" alt="A graduation cap and diploma resting on a stack of books" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" width="1200" height="550" loading="eager">
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-lg text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Most students only look at the two or three most famous scholarships for their target country, decide they're too competitive, and stop looking. That's a mistake — the majority of scholarship money isn't in those big-name awards at all, it's in smaller, less competitive, university-specific and country-specific funding that barely anyone applies for. Here's how to actually find it.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">How do I find scholarships I actually qualify for?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Start with your shortlisted universities directly, not a general search — most universities list international student scholarships on their own admissions page, and many are applied automatically once you're admitted. After that, check your destination country's official scholarship portal (government-funded schemes are usually less competitive than global name-brand awards), and finally look at subject-specific or employer-linked funding in your field. Working through universities first, then country, then subject, finds far more realistic options than searching "scholarships" generally.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Can I get a 100% scholarship to study abroad?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Full-ride scholarships exist but are genuinely rare and highly competitive — realistically, most students should plan around partial funding (anywhere from 10% to 75% of tuition) rather than counting on a full scholarship as their only funding plan. Combining a partial scholarship with a smaller student loan or family contribution is the far more common and realistic path.</p>
    <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl bg-bridge-50 p-5 border border-bridge-100">
      <p class="text-sm text-bridge-900"><strong>Quick tip:</strong> Apply for scholarships at the same time as your university application, not after. Many scholarship deadlines fall weeks before the general admission deadline, and applying late is the single most common reason students miss funding they were actually eligible for.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Do I need excellent grades to get a scholarship?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Not always. Merit-based scholarships do reward strong academic results, but need-based funding, country-specific grants, and awards for talent, leadership, or extracurricular achievement don't require top grades at all. A solid (not perfect) academic record combined with a well-written application is often enough for the less competitive schemes most students overlook.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">When should I start applying for scholarships?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">As early as possible — ideally at the same time you shortlist universities, which is often 8-12 months before your intake. Government and university scholarship deadlines regularly close before the general admission deadline, so waiting until you have an offer in hand can mean missing funding you would otherwise have qualified for.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Can I combine a scholarship with a student loan?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Yes, in almost all cases. A scholarship simply reduces the amount you need to fund separately, and most banks and loan providers are comfortable seeing a partial scholarship alongside a loan application — it can actually strengthen your case by lowering the total amount you need to borrow.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 border border-black/10 rounded-2xl divide-y divide-black/10 bg-white">
      <div>
        <button class="faq-btn w-full text-left px-6 py-4 font-display font-semibold text-bridge-900 flex justify-between items-center">Are scholarship applications separate from university applications?<span class="faq-icon transition-transform">+</span></button>
        <p class="faq-answer px-6 pb-5 text-sm text-ink/70">It depends. Many university scholarships are applied automatically based on your admission application, while government and external scholarships usually need a separate application with their own deadline.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <button class="faq-btn w-full text-left px-6 py-4 font-display font-semibold text-bridge-900 flex justify-between items-center">Do scholarships cover living costs, or just tuition?<span class="faq-icon transition-transform">+</span></button>
        <p class="faq-answer hidden px-6 pb-5 text-sm text-ink/70">Most scholarships cover tuition only, though some larger government-funded schemes also include a living stipend. Always check what's included before assuming it covers your full budget.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <button class="faq-btn w-full text-left px-6 py-4 font-display font-semibold text-bridge-900 flex justify-between items-center">Can I apply for scholarships in more than one country at once?<span class="faq-icon transition-transform">+</span></button>
        <p class="faq-answer hidden px-6 pb-5 text-sm text-ink/70">Yes — there's no rule against applying to multiple countries and universities in parallel, and doing so genuinely improves your overall chances of securing some funding.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <button class="faq-btn w-full text-left px-6 py-4 font-display font-semibold text-bridge-900 flex justify-between items-center">What makes a scholarship application stand out?<span class="faq-icon transition-transform">+</span></button>
        <p class="faq-answer hidden px-6 pb-5 text-sm text-ink/70">A specific, honest personal statement that explains why you chose that field and what you plan to do with it — reviewers read hundreds of generic essays, and a concrete, personal answer is what actually gets remembered.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl bg-dusk-gradient text-white p-8 text-center">
      <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Want us to check what you actually qualify for?</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-white/75 text-sm max-w-md mx-auto">Share your academic profile and destination — we'll match you with real funding options, free of charge.</p>
      <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
        <a href="/#lead-form" class="btn-primary nav-link inline-flex">Check Scholarship Eligibility</a>
        <a href="/scholarship" class="btn-secondary nav-link inline-flex">Explore Scholarships</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8"><a href="/blogs" class="nav-link text-sm font-semibold text-bridge-800 hover:underline">&larr; Back to all articles</a></p>
  </div>
</article>
